'''Fante''' ({{IPA|fat|ˈfɑnti|lang}}), also known as '''Fanti''', '''Fantse''', or '''Mfantse''', is one of the three [[literary dialect]]s of the [[Twi-Fante language]], along with [[Asante dialect|Asante]] and [[Akuapem dialect|Akuapem]] Twi, with which it is [[Mutual intelligibility|mutually intelligible]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite book|title=A Phonology of Akan: Akuapem, Asante, Fante|last1=Schacter|first1=Paul|last2=Fromkin|first2=Victoria|publisher=UC Press|year=1968|location=Los Angeles|pages=3}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last1=Arhin|first1=Kwame|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=R9gJAQAAIAAJ&q=twi|title=A Profile of Brong Kyempim: Essays on the Archaeology, History, Language and Politics of the Brong Peoples of Ghana|last2=Studies|first2=University of Ghana Institute of African|date=1979|publisher=Afram|language=en}}</ref> It is principally spoken in the central and southern regions of Ghana as well as in settlements in other regions in western Ghana, Ivory Coast, as well as in Liberia, Gambia and Angola.<ref name=e25/>

Fante is the common [[dialect]] of the [[Fante people]], whose communities each have their own [[Subdialect|subdialects]], namely Agona, Anomabo, Abura and Gomoa,<ref>{{e25|aka}}</ref> all of which are mutually intelligible. Schacter and Fromkin describe two main Fante dialect groups: Fante 1, which uses a syllable-final /w/ and thus distinguishes ''kaw'' ("dance") and ''ka'' ("bite"); and Fante 2, where these words are homophonous.<ref name=":1" /> A standardized form of Fante is taught in primary and secondary schools.<ref name=e25/> Many Fantes are [[bilingual]] or [[Bidialectism|bidialectal]] and most can speak [[Asante dialect|Asante]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Abakah|first=Emmanuel Nicholas|date=2004|title=Elision in Fante|url=https://www.utbildning.gu.se/digitalAssets/1324/1324075_elision-in-fante.pdf|journal=Africa & Asia|pages=181–213|archive-date=2019-12-25|access-date=2019-12-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191225200304/https://www.utbildning.gu.se/digitalAssets/1324/1324075_elision-in-fante.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> 

Notable speakers include [[Peter Turkson|Cardinal Peter Appiah Turkson]],<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/feb/11/next-pope-contenders-vatican-job|title=Who will be the next pope? The contenders for Vatican's top job|last1=Jones|first1=Sam|date=2013-02-11|work=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=2019-12-22|author2=[[Afua Hirsch]]|language=en-GB|issn=0261-3077}}</ref> [[Jane Naana Opoku-Agyemang]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-07-18 |title=Make no mistake, Jane Naana Opoku-Agyemang is a courageous Fante! |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/features/Make-no-mistake-Jane-Naana-Opoku-Agyemang-is-a-courageous-Fante-1010683 |access-date=2023-10-02 |website=GhanaWeb |language=en}}</ref> former United Nations Secretary General [[Kofi Annan]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.williamshawcross.com/index.php?page=annan|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140114194400/http://www.williamshawcross.com/index.php?page=annan|url-status=dead|archive-date=2014-01-14|title=William Shawcross - UK : official personal website|date=2014-01-14|access-date=2019-12-22}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/Kofi-Annan|title=Kofi Annan {{!}} Biography & Facts|website=Encyclopedia Britannica|language=en|access-date=2019-12-22}}</ref> and former Ghanaian presidents [[Kwame Nkrumah]] and [[John Atta Mills]].<ref>{{Cite book|title=A Descriptive Grammar of Fanti|last=Welmers|first=William Everett|publisher=Linguistic Society of America|year=1946|pages=7}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.ngrguardiannews.com/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=93356:john-atta-mills-death-of-an-african-leader&catid=73:policy-a-politics&Itemid=607|location=Lagos, Nigeria|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130601233735/http://www.ngrguardiannews.com/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=93356:john-atta-mills-death-of-an-african-leader&catid=73:policy-a-politics&Itemid=607|url-status=dead|archive-date=2013-06-01|title=John Atta Mills: Death of