Today Fante is spoken by more than 6 million people in Ghana primarily in the Central and Western Regions. It is also widely spoken in Tema, where majority of the people in that city are native Fante speakers who were settled after the new port was built. 

One striking characteristic of the Fante dialect is its level of adaptability due to British colonial influence and "to fill lexical and semantic gaps, for reasons of simplicity. Examples of such borrowings include ''rɛkɔso'' ("records"), ''rɔba'' "rubber", ''nɔma'' ("number"), ''kolapuse'' "collapse", and ''dɛkuleti'' "decorate".<ref name=":3">{{Cite journal|last1=Apenteng|first1=Monica Amoah|last2=Amfo|first2=Nana Aba Appiah|date=2014|title=The Form and Function of English Loanwords in Akan|url=https://www.njas.fi/njas/article/view/138|journal=Nordic Journal of African Studies|volume=23|pages=219–240}}</ref> Native names are occasionally anglicized, such as "Mεnsa" becoming "Mensah" or "Atta" becoming "Arthur".<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Agyekum|first=Kofi|date=2006-12-31|title=The Sociolinguistic of Akan Personal Names|url=https://njas.fi/njas/article/view/24|journal=Nordic Journal of African Studies|language=en|volume=15|issue=2|issn=1459-9465}}</ref>

== Etymology ==
The name "[[Fante people|Fante]]" has different proposed origins.

The first and most widely accepted explanation comes from oral tradition. It holds that the [[Fante people]] separated from the [[Akan people|other Akan groups]] around 1250 AD in the area now known as [[Brong-Ahafo region|Brong Ahafo]]. Their departure is believed to have given rise to the name Fante, derived from the phrase "''Fa-atsew''" meaning "''the half that left''". This split is believed to have occurred at a place called Krako, present day [[Techiman]] in the [[Bono East Region|Bono East]] Region of [[Ghana]]. From this point, the Fante developed as a distinct [[Akan people|Akan]] group. The [[Fante people]] were led by three great warriors known as Obrumankoma, the whale;  Odapagyan, the eagle; and Oson, the elephant.  These names continue to hold cultural and symbolic importance among the [[Fante people|Fante]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Shumway |first=Rebecca |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781580467391/type/book |title=The Fante and the Transatlantic Slave Trade |date=2011-10-15 |publisher=Boydell and Brewer Limited |isbn=978-1-58046-739-1 |edition=1 |pages=31 |doi=10.1017/9781580467391}}</ref>

The second explanation suggests that the name Fante comes from a difference in food habits between them and [[Asante people|Asante]]. According to this version, the [[Fante people|Fante]] were known for eating spinach, called ''efan'', while the [[Asante people|Asante]] ate another herb known as ''san''.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iJByDwAAQBAJ|title=Landscapes, Sources and Intellectual Projects of the West African Past: Essays in Honour of Paulo Fernando de Moraes Farias|date=2018-07-26|publisher=BRILL|isbn=978-90-04-38018-9|language=en}}</ref>{{Page needed|date=June 2025}}

This theory is not only inconsistent in linguistics but also historically flawed. The Asante did not emerge as a major political force until the late seventeenth century, by which time the Fante were already established and known. The contrast between Fante and Asante identities only began to develop in the latter part of the eighteenth century, making any connection between the name Fante and the Asante anachronistic. For these reasons, the first origin story is considered more reliable, both in terms of language and historical accuracy.{{CN|date=June 2025}}

